# Final Results Summary

## 1. WS-4000 Baseline Results

The main synthetic network used for comparison was the Watts–Strogatz network with N = 4000 nodes.

- Average degree = 4.00
- Clustering coefficient = 0.1764
- Average path length = 7.5644
- Infection rate (β) = 0.40
- Recovery rate (γ) = 0.20
- Basic reproduction number (R0) = 2.00

### SIR Results

- Average peak infection = 1770.40 nodes
- 95% CI = [1668.86, 1871.94]
- Average time to peak = 16.78
- Average final epidemic size = 3799.32 nodes

## 2. SNAP-4039 Baseline Results

The real-world validation used the SNAP Facebook network containing 4039 nodes and 88234 edges.

The final comparison evaluated the structural properties and SIR spreading behaviour of the SNAP network against the WS-4000 synthetic network.

### Baseline Comparison

| Network | Average Peak | Average Time to Peak | Average Final Size |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| WS-4000 | 1770.40 | 16.78 | 3799.32 |
| SNAP-4039 | 2538.72 | 6.32 | 3930.82 |

## 3. Network-Size Analysis

Synthetic networks were evaluated for:

- N = 100
- N = 250
- N = 500
- N = 1000
- N = 2000
- N = 4000

The absolute number of infected nodes increased with network size, while the percentage of nodes infected at the peak remained broadly stable.

## 4. Network Topology Analysis

The effect of rewiring probability was evaluated using p = 0, 0.3, and 1.

| Rewiring Probability | Average Peak Infection |
|---:|---:|
| 0.00 | 12.32 |
| 0.30 | 1692.72 |
| 1.00 | 1994.24 |

The results show that increasing network randomness and introducing shortcuts substantially increased the spreading potential.

## 5. Intervention Results

### WS-4000

| Intervention | Peak Reduction | Final Size Reduction |
|---|---:|---:|
| Random removal | 8.90% | 3.73% |
| Degree-based removal | 22.85% | 8.85% |
| Betweenness-based removal | 26.69% | 14.00% |

### SNAP-4039

| Intervention | Peak Reduction |
|---|---:|
| Random removal | 7.18% |
| Degree-based removal | 24.73% |
| Betweenness-based removal | 82.88% |

## 6. Main Finding

The final experiments indicate that network structure has a strong influence on misinformation spreading. Betweenness-based node removal produced the largest reduction in peak infection among the evaluated intervention strategies, particularly in the SNAP-4039 network.

These results support the use of network structural information as a complementary approach to content-based misinformation moderation.
